WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.780
What do you think is the most
revolutionary development in language

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:05.760
learning in the last 50 years?

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:09.930
In my opinion, and I've been learning
for languages for more than 50

00:00:09.935 --> 00:00:14.010
years, I would say it's the MP3
file, and I'm gonna explain why.

00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:18.000
There are other things that have
revolutionized language learning:

00:00:18.390 --> 00:00:22.725
the internet, online dictionaries
uh, and so on and so on.

00:00:23.535 --> 00:00:25.815
But to me, the big thing is the MP3 file.

00:00:26.295 --> 00:00:34.365
And I remember when I was a student
in France, I was paid by a language

00:00:34.370 --> 00:00:40.515
school in Paris to run their language
lab for English 30 people sitting

00:00:40.635 --> 00:00:46.695
and listening to the same content
on these open reel tape recorders.

00:00:47.144 --> 00:00:52.155
I remember sitting in Hong Kong in my room
listening to an open reel tape recorder.

00:00:52.155 --> 00:00:53.385
I couldn't take it anywhere.

00:00:53.894 --> 00:01:00.989
Today on my cell phone I have an
enormous library, you know, a language

00:01:00.989 --> 00:01:06.840
lab of content in so many different
languages, all consisting of MP3 files.

00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:13.199
I can share MP3 files, I can send
them to my friends, I can download

00:01:13.205 --> 00:01:16.350
them from YouTube, from podcasts.

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:17.470
They're everywhere.

00:01:18.375 --> 00:01:24.045
And what's more, the technology is
continuing to evolve so that now

00:01:24.045 --> 00:01:28.995
I'm able to get an MP3 file, import
the audio to LingQ and automatically

00:01:28.995 --> 00:01:30.885
create a transcript and a lesson.

00:01:31.755 --> 00:01:38.085
What's more, if I use our audio
import system, the transcript is

00:01:38.145 --> 00:01:42.835
timestamped, which has a tremendous
advantages advantage for us at LingQ.

00:01:42.855 --> 00:01:47.655
It does at least two things: one,
if I study the lesson in sentence

00:01:47.655 --> 00:01:53.625
view, then I have the natural audio
timestamped to the sentence, which

00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:57.555
is very handy, especially if you are
at an early stage in the language.

00:01:57.855 --> 00:02:01.935
Later on, I can listen to the audio
and read and follow along, or listen,

00:02:02.205 --> 00:02:04.035
you know, away from the computer.

00:02:04.285 --> 00:02:08.785
But initially to get started to get
some traction, it's very helpful to have

00:02:08.785 --> 00:02:13.795
the audio for that sentence accurately
timestamped to match the sentence.

00:02:14.215 --> 00:02:17.805
Another thing I can do on
LingQ is use our kara...

00:02:18.345 --> 00:02:24.105
what we call karaoke view so that I
can turn the audio on and the text

00:02:24.109 --> 00:02:27.525
will scroll down following the audio.

00:02:27.825 --> 00:02:33.675
If you've never used LingQ before, a LingQ
lesson allows you to read through and

00:02:33.675 --> 00:02:39.375
listen and also watch, in this case, to a
piece of content in English, translating

00:02:39.375 --> 00:02:43.290
the words and phrases that you don't
know, saving them to your own personal

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:47.550
database and then learning English from
content you're actually interested in.

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:53.160
That's also very helpful because it
introduces another way of studying the

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:57.720
text and you know, every time we're
learning anything for that matter,

00:02:58.665 --> 00:03:03.645
particularly languages, if we can cover
the same material in different ways, you

00:03:03.645 --> 00:03:11.715
know, sentence view or full text, or vocab
review, or karaoke view, or listening away

00:03:11.865 --> 00:03:17.954
from the lesson, all of this is covering
the same material in different ways.

00:03:17.954 --> 00:03:20.625
It introduces a certain degree of variety.

00:03:20.950 --> 00:03:25.180
Because we know that, you know, block
learning is not as effective as when

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:29.140
we have what's often, often called
interleaving, where we are covering the

00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:32.830
same subject and inter interspersing
other things with it and so forth.

00:03:32.830 --> 00:03:37.640
So again, the MP3 file makes sentence
view possible, makes karaoke view

00:03:37.660 --> 00:03:41.320
possible, makes it possible to
take it away and listen elsewhere.

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:42.240
It.

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:44.550
It's just a wonderful, wonderful thing.

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:50.040
I am constantly on the lookout for good
MP3 resources for different languages.

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:55.920
Obviously, podcasts potentially are a good
source, but very often the podcast won't

00:03:55.920 --> 00:04:01.980
let you download the MP3 file because they
want to get you to go to their podcast

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:04.890
and build up their, you know, viewer base.

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:08.880
Uh, we're dealing with this at
LingQ so that we are still able

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:13.050
to access those audio files and
create lessons where we essentially

00:04:13.055 --> 00:04:14.940
refer people back to the podcast.

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:18.090
But some podcasts do let
you take the MP3 file.

00:04:18.780 --> 00:04:24.570
And, uh, I've been doing, for example,
MTV Lebanon, which is a TV station

00:04:24.570 --> 00:04:26.400
in Lebanon for Levantine Arabic.

00:04:26.735 --> 00:04:30.395
Some of their podcasts let you
download the MP3 file or convert

00:04:30.395 --> 00:04:33.005
it to an MP3 file and some don't.

00:04:33.010 --> 00:04:37.955
I don't know why I even signed up 'cause
I had downloaded some of their MP3 files.

00:04:38.285 --> 00:04:39.305
It's kinda interesting.

00:04:39.485 --> 00:04:41.975
So I pay them their $35 a year.

00:04:41.975 --> 00:04:44.585
I don't wanna feel as if I'm
taking advantage of them.

00:04:45.260 --> 00:04:50.390
However, when you sign up, they ask you
for your age and they have it in different

00:04:50.390 --> 00:04:54.530
age classes, and the maximum age is 75.

00:04:54.530 --> 00:04:56.810
So they have a group 70 to 75.

00:04:56.960 --> 00:05:01.549
They got nothing for anyone over 75,
so I kind of snuck in there as the 70

00:05:01.549 --> 00:05:06.739
to 75 age group, paid my 25 bucks for
the year, whatever the number was, and

00:05:06.739 --> 00:05:12.369
now I feel better about downloading
their MP3 files for import into LingQ.

00:05:12.859 --> 00:05:19.815
But, If people know of good MP3 file
resources for different languages,

00:05:19.815 --> 00:05:23.234
even if there's no transcript, if there
is a transcript, so much the better.

00:05:23.385 --> 00:05:27.255
But if there's no transcript, these
can be very valuable for LingQ members

00:05:27.255 --> 00:05:33.164
and perhaps for other learners, uh,
because it's possible then to get through

00:05:33.164 --> 00:05:38.025
Whisper AI accurate timestamping, which
is very helpful in language learning.

00:05:38.115 --> 00:05:39.315
So there you have it.

00:05:39.645 --> 00:05:41.835
The MP3 revolution.

00:05:42.075 --> 00:05:44.685
Let me know what you think and
let me know if you have any

00:05:44.685 --> 00:05:50.355
suggestions as to, uh, resources
where people can download MP3 files.

00:05:51.105 --> 00:05:51.735
Thanks for listening.

00:05:51.740 --> 00:05:51.945
Bye.

